elixiraotc compiles an Elixir script (.exs) into a single, self-contained
native executable. The result embeds a statically linked, JIT-enabled
Erlang/OTP emulator plus the Elixir, Mix, IEx and Hex libraries, so it runs
on a machine with no Erlang or Elixir installed. The BEAM code is compiled
ahead of time and recorded into the executable, so startup skips the usual
JIT warm-up.

Usage

    elixiraotc build hello.exs -o hello
    ./hello

`build` compiles hello.exs into the executable `hello`. `run FILE.exs [ARG]`
builds to a temporary file, executes it with the given arguments and discards
it.

What is embedded, and when

The executable embeds the runtime and the standard library: the OTP emulator,
Elixir, Mix, IEx and Hex. It does not embed the dependencies referenced by the
script.

`Mix.install/2` is not evaluated while the script is compiled, so a script
that uses it compiles with warnings that its `Mix.install/2`-provided modules
are undefined. That is expected. The call runs when the resulting executable
runs, at which point Mix downloads and compiles those dependencies from Hex
exactly as `elixir` would on first use, so that first run still needs network
access (or a pre-warmed ~/.hex / ~/.mix cache). Scripts that avoid
`Mix.install/2` run fully offline.

Examples under %%PREFIX%%/share/examples/elixiraotc/:

    hello.exs    dependency-free; runs fully offline
    nanoid.exs   uses Mix.install/2; fetches nanoid from Hex on first run

    static_nif/  C and rustler NIFs linked statically via `elixiraotc build
                 FILE.exs --native NATIVE.json`; see static_nif/README.md

This port reproduces the upstream single-binary build from the pinned
Erlang/OTP and Elixir commits, rebuilding every patched preloaded module from
source rather than shipping precompiled artifacts.
